I. The Operations (The "Push_swap" Model)

Instead of standard array swaps, you must use these specific moves:

    Push (pa, pb): Move the top element from one stack to another.

    Swap (sa, sb, ss): Swap the top two elements.

    Rotate (ra, rb, rr): Shift all elements up; the top becomes the bottom.

    Reverse Rotate (rra, rrb, rrr): Shift all elements down; the bottom becomes the top.

II. The Complexity Requirements

You must implement four distinct strategies and select them at runtime using flags (e.g., --simple, --medium).
Strategy	Complexity	Suggested Algorithms
Simple	O(n2)	Selection Sort (find min, push to b) or Bubble Sort.
Medium	O(nn​)	Chunk-based sorting (divide stack into n​ ranges).
Complex	O(nlogn)	Radix Sort (using bits) or Quicksort partitioning.
Adaptive	Variable	Uses the Disorder Metric to pick one of the above.
III. The Disorder Metric (Mandatory)

Before moving any numbers, you must calculate how "messy" the stack is.

    Formula: Total PairsMistakes​.

    A "mistake" is whenever a larger number appears before a smaller number (a[i]>a[j] where i<j).

    Thresholds for Adaptive Mode:

        Disorder < 0.2: Use O(n2).

        0.2 ≤ Disorder < 0.5: Use O(nn​).

        Disorder ≥ 0.5: Use O(nlogn).

IV. What to Learn to Accomplish This
1. Data Structures (Linked Lists vs Arrays)

While you can use arrays, Doubly Linked Lists are often easier for Push_swap because "rotating" a list is just a pointer swap, and "pushing" is just adding/removing a node.
2. Algorithmic Thinking

You aren't just sorting; you are minimizing the number of moves.

    For O(n2): Learn how to efficiently find the smallest number and bring it to the top using the shortest path (either ra or rra).

    For O(nn​): Learn about "Chunks." You push numbers within a certain range to stack b first to keep them roughly sorted before pushing them back.

3. Bit Manipulation (For Radix Sort)

If you choose Radix Sort for your O(nlogn) strategy, you will need to learn how to check the i-th bit of a number ((num >> i) & 1) to decide whether to push it to stack b or rotate it.
4. Error Handling and Parsing

Your program must handle:

    Non-integer arguments.

    Duplicates (e.g., ./push_swap 1 2 1 should trigger an Error).

    Integers outside the INT_MIN to INT_MAX range.

V. Recommended Roadmap

    Parsing: Write a robust parser that converts argv into a stack (Linked List) and checks for errors.

    Operations: Implement the 11 basic operations (sa, pb, ra, etc.).

    Disorder Function: Implement the compute_disorder logic exactly as described in the subject.

    The Algorithms: Start with the Simple O(n2) version, then move to Complex (Radix is usually the easiest O(nlogn) to implement with stacks).

    Benchmark Mode: Implement the --bench flag to output statistics to stderr.

